{"sounds":{"intro":"This is Early Modern English, the language of Shakespeare (about 1590 to 1616), shown in the modern spelling used in school editions. Read each word as you would modern English, then learn the old forms.","rows":[["th","in \"thou\", \"thee\", \"thy\", \"thine\" it is the soft \"th\" of \"this\"","thou"],["-est","the ending for \"thou\": thou lovest, thou hast, thou art","lovest"],["-eth","the old ending for \"he / she / it\": he loveth, he hath, he doth","loveth"],["'","an apostrophe shows a dropped letter: 'tis (it is), o'er (over), e'en (even), ne'er (never)","'tis"],["wh-","wherefore means \"why\", not \"where\". Whence = from where. Whither = to where","wherefore"],["-ed","often spoken as a separate syllable in verse: \"blessed\", \"loved\"","loved"]],"notes":["\"Thou\" is for one person, informally (a friend, a child, a servant, God). \"You\" is polite, or plural. Shakespeare uses the choice for effect: switching from \"you\" to \"thou\" can be an insult or a sign of love.","Modern things (phone, wifi, computer, train, bicycle, airport) did not exist. Where there is a period word it is used (coach for bus, hackney for taxi). Where not, the modern noun is kept inside Early Modern grammar, and it is not an attested line.","This is a teaching language: Early Modern grammar and vocabulary, not quotations. Real Elizabethan spelling and pronunciation varied. Stress is as in modern English."]},"numbers":[{"id":"0-to-10","title":"0 to 10","items":[["zero","naught"],["one","one"],["two","two"],["three","three"],["four","four"],["five","five"],["six","six"],["seven","seven"],["eight","eight"],["nine","nine"],["ten","ten"]]},{"id":"11-to-20","title":"11 to 20","items":[["eleven","eleven"],["twelve","twelve"],["thirteen","thirteen"],["fourteen","fourteen"],["fifteen","fifteen"],["sixteen","sixteen"],["seventeen","seventeen"],["eighteen","eighteen"],["nineteen","nineteen"],["twenty","a score"]]},{"id":"tens","title":"Tens","items":[["ten","ten"],["twenty