e schoolmasters"],["I am happy","I am glad"],["he is a doctor","He is a physician"],["you all are late","Ye are come late"]]},{"id":"thou-thee-thy-thine","title":"Thou, thee, thy, thine","text":["\"Thou\" changes like \"I, me, my, mine\": thou (subject), thee (object), thy (your, before a consonant), thine (your, before a vowel or \"h\", and when it stands alone)."],"rows":[["thou (subject)","Thou art my friend"],["thee (object)","I love thee"],["thy (before a consonant)","thy book"],["thine (before a vowel, or alone)","thine eyes, the book is thine"]],"ex":[["thou art my friend","Thou art my friend"],["I love thee","I love thee"],["this is thy book","This is thy book"],["the book is thine","The book is thine"],["thine eyes are fair","Thine eyes are fair"],["I give it to thee","I give it to thee"],["art thou well?","Art thou well?"],["I thank thee","I thank thee"]]},{"id":"singular-and-plural","title":"Singular and plural","text":["The plural is -s or -es, as today. A few old plurals survive: kine (cows), shoon (shoes), eyne (eyes), brethren (brothers). After a number the noun is plural."],"rows":[["-s","book → books"],["-es","glass → glasses"],["old plurals","kine, shoon, eyne, brethren"],["after a number","two quills"]],"ex":[["the boy","the lad"],["the girl","the maid"],["the books","the books"],["a red house","a red house"],["the cities","the cities"],["one cat, two cats","one cat, two cats"],["one pen, two pens","one quill, two quills"],["a lesson, lessons","a lesson, lessons"]]},{"id":"to-be","title":"To be","text":["I am, thou art, he is, we are, ye are, they are. Past: I was, thou wast (or wert), he was, we were. \"'Tis\" = it is."],"rows":[["I am","I am"],["you are (thou)","thou art"],["he / she is","he is / she is"],["we are","we are"],["you all are","ye are"],["they are","they are"]],"ex":[["I am English","I am an Englishman"],["he is a doctor","He is a physician"],["it is Monday","'Tis Monday"],["we are from Uzbekistan","We are of Uzbekistan"],["they are tall","They are tall"],["I am at home","I am at home"],["she is tired","She is weary"],["where are you?","Where art thou?"],["we are well","We are well"],["the soup is cold","The broth is cold"]]},{"id":"to-have","title":"To have","text":["I have, thou hast, he hath, we have, ye have, they have. \"Hath\" is the old \"has\"."],"rows":[["I have","I have"],["you have (thou)","thou hast"],["he / she has","he hath"],["we have","we have"],["they have","they have"]],"ex":[["I have a brother","I have a brother"],["she has two cats","She hath two cats"],["we have time","We have time"],["I am hungry","I am hungry"],["they have a car","They have a carriage"],["there is a lesson today","There is a lesson this day"],["there is no teacher","There is no schoolmaster"],["I have a question","I have a question to ask"]]},{"id":"verbs-present-tense","title":"Verbs: present tense","text":["After \"thou\" add -est (thou lovest). After \"he / she / it\" the old ending is -eth (he loveth). For \"have\" and \"do\": hath and doth. The other persons do not change."],"rows":[["I work","I labour"],["you work (thou)","thou labourest"],["he / she works","he laboureth"],["we work","we labour"],["you (formal) work","you labour"],["they work","they labour"]],"ex":[["I drink water","I drink water"],["he writes a letter","He writeth a letter"],["we study Latin","We study Latin"],["they play football","They play football"],["she reads a book","She readeth a book"],["I know the answer","I know the answer"],["you speak English","Thou speakest English"],["I live in Tashkent","I dwell in Tashkent"]]},{"id":"going-to-go","title":"Going: to go","text":["I go, thou goest, he goeth, we go, ye go, they go. \"Whither\" = to where. \"Hither\" = to here. \"Thither\" = to there."],"rows":[["I go","I go"],["you go (thou)","thou goest"],["he / she goes","he goeth"],["we go","we go"],["they go","they go"]],"ex":[["I am going to school","I go to school"],["where are you going?","Whither goest thou?"],["we are going to eat","We go to dinner"],["she is going to travel","She goeth to travel"],["let us go!","Come, let us go"],["they go home","They go home"],["I go to the market","I go to the market"],["he goes to London","He goeth to London"]]},{"id":"basic-sentences","title":"Basic sentences","text":["The order is mostly as today: subject, verb, object. But Shakespeare may put the verb first for effect: \"Speak I not true?\""],"rows":[["subject + verb + object","I eat bread"],["adjective + noun","a great house"],["add time or place","He laboureth here this day"]],"ex":[["I eat bread","I eat bread"],["Maria reads a book","Maria readeth a book"],["we live in a big house","We dwell in a great house"],["he works here today","He laboureth here this day"],["the dog is black","The hound is black"]]},{"id":"negation","title":"Negation","text":["Early Modern English puts \"not\" after the verb: I know not. There is no \"do not\" needed. Also \"no\" and \"never\". Always compare with the positive form."],"rows":[["not (after the verb)","I know not"],["no (before a noun)","I have no carriage"],["never","I never lie"],["neither ... nor","neither he nor she"]],"ex":[["I want","I desire it"],["I do not want","I desire it not"],["I speak English","I speak English"],["I do not speak English","I speak not English"],["I like it","I like it"],["I do not like it","I like it not"],["she is here","She is here"],["she is not here","She is not here"],["I have a car","I have a carriage"],["I do not have a car","I have no carriage"],["I understand","I understand"],["I do not understand","I understand not"],["we are tired","We are weary"],["we are not tired","We are not weary"],["he works on Sundays","He laboureth on Sundays"],["he does not work on Sundays","He laboureth not on Sundays"],["I know","I know"],["I do not know","I know not"]]},{"id":"asking-questions","title":"Asking questions","text":["Turn the verb and subject round: \"Art thou weary?\", \"Hast thou a quill?\". Shakespeare also uses \"dost thou\". Answer \"Ay\" or \"Nay\"."],"rows":[["statement","Thou art weary."],["question","Art thou weary?"],["answer yes / no","Ay / Nay"]],"ex":[["do you speak English?","Speakest thou English?"],["is she a doctor?","Is she a physician?"],["do you have a pen?","Hast thou a quill?"],["are you tired?","Art thou weary?"],["is it far?","Is it far?"],["is the restaurant open?","Is the tavern open?"]]},{"id":"question-words","title":"Question words","text":["\"Wherefore\" means \"why\". \"Whence\" is from where. \"Whither\" is to where."],"rows":[["what","what"],["who","who"],["where","where"],["whence (from where)","whence"],["whither (to where)","whither"],["when","when"],["why","wherefore"],["how","how"],["which","which"],["how much / how many","how much / how many"]],"ex":[["what is this?","What is this?"],["who is she?","Who is she?"],["where do you live?","Where dwellest thou?"],["when is the exam?","When is the examination?"],["why are you late?","Wherefore art thou late?"],["how much is it?","At what price?"],["which book?","Which book?"],["where are you from?","Whence comest thou?"],["where are you going?","Whither goest thou?"]]},{"id":"possessives","title":"Possessives","text":["My, thy, his, her, our, your, their. Before a vowel or \"h\", the old \"mine\" and \"thine\" are used: mine eyes, thine own."],"rows":[["my","my / mine"],["your (thou)","thy / thine"],["his / her","his / her"],["our","our"],["your (formal)","your"],["their","their"]],"ex":[["my house","my house"],["your friends","thy friends"],["his sister","his sister"],["our teacher","our schoolmaster"],["their car","their carriage"],["mine own heart","mine own heart"]]},{"id":"formal-vs-informal","title":"Formal vs informal","text":["\"Thou\" is close or lower. \"You\" is polite. \"Sir\", \"madam\" and \"my lord\" are used for respect."],"rows":[["you (informal)","thou"],["you (formal)","you"],["sir (polite)","sir / my lord"]],"ex":[["how are you? (informal)","How fares it with thee?"],["how are you? (formal)","How fare you?"],["thank you, sir","I thank you, sir"],["excuse me, madam","By your leave, madam"],["please sit down","Pray you, sit"]]},{"id":"past-and-future-simple","title":"Past and future (simple)","text":["Past: add -ed (laboured), or use the old forms (did, was, went). Future: \"shall\" is the plain future. \"Will\" means want, or intend."],"rows":[["I worked","I laboured"],["you worked (thou)","thou didst labour"],["he / she worked","he laboured"],["I will work","I shall labour"],["we will work","we shall labour"]],"ex":[["yesterday I worked","Yesterday I laboured"],["she bought a book","She bought a book"],["I will call you tomorrow","I shall call thee tomorrow"],["we will eat at eight","We shall dine at eight"],["they travelled to Spain","They journeyed into Spain"],["I went to the market","I went to the market"],["he will come later","He shall come anon"]]},{"id":"commands","title":"Commands","text":["The command is the plain verb. For \"do not\" add \"not\" after it: Come not hither. \"Thee\" often follows: Sit thee down."],"rows":[["come! (informal)","Come hither"],["do not come!","Come not hither"],["come! (formal)","Pray you, come hither"]],"ex":[["come here","Come hither"],["do not come here","Come not hither"],["sit down","Sit thee down"],["do not sit down","Sit not down"],["be quiet","Hold thy peace"],["do not be late","Be not late"],["open the door","Open the door"],["close the window","Shut the casement"],["listen to me","Hear me"],["do not worry","Fear not"],["speak slowly","Speak slow"],["wait a moment","Stay a while"]]},{"id":"can-must-want","title":"Can, must, want","text":["Can (able), may (permitted), must, will (want to), would. \"Thou canst\", \"thou mayst\", \"thou must\", \"thou wilt\"."],"rows":[["I can","I can / thou canst"],["I must","I must / thou must"],["I want","I would / thou wouldst"]],"ex":[["I can swim","I can swim"],["I cannot swim","I cannot swim"],["can you help me?","Canst thou help me?"],["we must go","We must go"],["we must not go","We must not go"],["I want to eat","I would eat"],["I do not want to eat","I would not eat"],["you can come","Thou mayst come"],["you cannot come","Thou canst not come"],["she wants to study","She would study"],["I must work today","I must labour this day"],["you must not run","Thou must not run"]]},{"id":"in-on-at","title":"In, on, at","text":["On = upon. Under = beneath, 'neath. Next to = hard by. Near = nigh. Over = o'er. Between = betwixt."],"rows":[["in / at","in / at"],["to","to / unto"],["on","upon"],["under","beneath / 'neath"],["next to","hard by"]],"ex":[["the book is on the table","The book is upon the table"],["the cat is under the table","The cat is beneath the table"],["I am at school","I am at school"],["I am at home","I am at home"],["the shop is next to the bank","The shop is hard by the bank"],["the park is near the station","The park is nigh the coaching inn"],["we meet in the morning","We meet i' the morning"],["the lesson is at nine","The lesson is at nine"],["the key is in the bag","The key is in the bag"]]},{"id":"comparing-things","title":"Comparing things","text":["Add -er and -est, or use more and most. Shakespeare may double them: the most unkindest cut. Than uses the subject form: taller than I."],"rows":[["bigger than","greater than"],["better","better"],["the biggest","the greatest"]],"ex":[["big, bigger, the biggest","great, greater, the greatest"],["small, smaller, the smallest","small, smaller, the smallest"],["this is better than that","This is better than that"],["this is worse than that","This is worse than that"],["she is taller than me","She is taller than I"],["Tashkent is bigger than Samarkand","Tashkent is greater than Samarkand"],["it is the cheapest","It is the most cheap"],["the same as","the very same as"]]},{"id":"moods-and-wishes","title":"Moods and wishes","text":["A wish uses \"would\": Would I were a bird! A condition uses \"an\" or \"if\": An thou wilt, come. After \"if\" the verb may be in the subjunctive: If it rain."],"rows":[["wish","Would I were..."],["if","an / if"],["let us","Let us..."]],"ex":[["if it rain, I will stay at home","If it rain, I will stay at home"],["let us go","Let us go"],["I wish I were a bird","Would I were a bird"],["God be with you","God be with you"],["if thou wilt, come","An thou wilt, come"],["pray you, come in","Pray you, come in"]]},{"id":"everyday-sentences","title":"Everyday sentences","text":[],"rows":[],"ex":[["he is a teacher","He is a schoolmaster"],["you (plural) are students","Ye are scholars"],["I am happy","I am glad"],["she is at school","She is at school"],["you are my friend","Thou art my friend"],["it is cold","'Tis cold"],["I study English","I study English"],["he teaches maths","He teacheth arithmetic"],["they work every day","They labour each day"],["is this your book?","Is this thy book?"],["are they here?","Are they here?"],["do you like tea?","Likest thou tea?"],["what time is it?","What is't o'clock?"],["whose book is this?","Whose book is this?"],["how old are you?","How old art thou?"],["how do you say it?","How is it said?"],["your book (formal)","your book"],["their house","their house"],["my phone","my phone"],["excuse me, sir","By your leave, sir"],["please sit down (formal)","Pray you, sit"],["come here (informal)","Come hither"],["yesterday I was tired","Yesterday I was weary"],["I will study tomorrow","I shall study tomorrow"],["we went to the park","We went to the park"]]}],"name":"Shakespearean English","id":"shk","flag":"en","note":""}